now_normal":0.0},"titles":{"year":"2026 so far: 10 daily record highs, 6 daily record lows","records":"Jacksonville is setting record highs far faster than chance","change":"Jacksonville's nights cooled over the long run but have warmed since 1986. Its days have warmed.","rain":"Rain: no clear long-term change"},"text":{"lede":"Yesterday's high of 69\u00b0F was typical for November 13.","t_yest":"November 13 was a typical day","p_yest":"The high of <strong>69\u00b0F</strong> was typical for November 13. The normal high for the date is 73\u00b0F, and the record is 87\u00b0F (2018). The low of <strong>46\u00b0F</strong> was typical for November 13.","t_rec":"Jacksonville is setting record highs far faster than chance","p_rec":"If Jacksonville's climate weren't changing, every year would have the same chance of holding a daily record. Since 2000, Jacksonville has set <strong>92 standing record lows</strong>, more than the 63 that chance alone would give, and <strong class=\"sig\">140 record highs</strong>, far more than the 63 that chance alone would give. The 2010s hold the most record highs of any decade (62). Jacksonville's all-time high is 104\u00b0F, on July 28, 1872.","t_chg":"Jacksonville's nights cooled over the long run but have warmed since 1986. Its days have warmed.","p_chg":"Across 154 complete years: Jacksonville's average low has fallen <strong class='sig'>\u22120.27\u00b0F per decade</strong> (95% range \u22120.32 to \u22120.21), about \u22124.1\u00b0F since 1872. Jacksonville's average high has risen <strong class='sig'>+0.20\u00b0F per decade</strong> (95% range +0.16 to +0.24), about +3.0\u00b0F since 1872. <strong>The last 40 years tell a different story:</strong> since 1986, lows have risen <strong class='sig'>+0.43\u00b0F per decade</strong> (95% range +0.07 to +0.80) and highs have risen <strong class='sig'>+0.42\u00b0F per decade</strong> (95% range +0.11 to +0.72). The full record joins different observing sites over the years, and a move (for example, from the city to a cooler airport) can hide or exaggerate a long-run trend, so the recent decades are the clearer guide here.","t_mon":"Lows rose in none; 12 months fell. Highs rose in 11 of 12 months.","t_rain":"Rain: no clear long-term change","p_rain":"Jacksonville's yearly precipitation shows no clear long-term change (+0.10 inches per decade, a range that includes zero). The 1991\u20132020 normal is 53.4 inches a year. The wettest year was 1947 (82.27 in); the driest, 1927 (30.44 in). Jacksonville almost never sees snow (the 1991\u20132020 average is 0.0 inches a winter), so there's no snowfall chart.","show_snow":false,"sig_bars":{"lo":false,"hi":true},"t_year":"2026 so far: 10 daily record highs, 6 daily record lows"}}